## 1. Introduction
Whatever the category they belong to, designing, synthesising and studying multifunctional materials that have sophisticated properties is an appealing challenge for both chemists and physicists. It requires a truly interdisciplinary approach to convert concepts into matter. Molecular chemistry can rely on the versatility of the building blocks combined with a rational design based on the self-assembly rules7 and the mastering of the exchange interaction6 or anisotropy8 to program the synthesis of such materials combining several physico-chemical properties. The superposition of different properties (Fig. 1(a)) can be considered as a first stage. Reaching the other cases (Fig. 1(b–e)) is a more demanding approach since it implies the introduction of a control of the interaction between the properties and/or towards an external stimulus. Creating and optimising such interactions is very stimulating because it allows to evidence new physico-chemical effects.
