ECM has drawn the attention of our community for at least two reasons. The first one is to be found in the everlasting fascination of chemists for chiral objects and for their synthesis in enantiomerically pure forms. Historically this interest was often centred on organic molecules and on drugs.12,13 It has now invested all the fields of chemistry including organometallic and coordination compounds,14–17 metal nanoparticles,18 and molecular materials.19 The second one is undoubtedly the prevision20,21 and observation in paramagnetic22,23 and diamagnetic24 systems of a cross-effect that occurs only in enantiopure magnetised objects. This so-called magneto-chiral (MCh) effectz being proportional to the magnetisation (cf. Section 3.2), its intensity was forecast to be greatly enhanced in ECM.
The first part of this review will be devoted to the different strategies developed to synthesise ECM. The synthetic pathways towards ECM somehow resemble those adopted for chiral metal–organic frameworks (MOF).17,19 Five strategies towards enantiopure chiral magnets can be identified (Fig. 3):
rely on spontaneous resolution process starting from achiral building blocks;
use chiral ligands bearing unpaired electrons (chiral radicals);
use chiral bridging ligands from the chiral pool; associate the chiral coligand with achiral connectors to build chiral extended networks; master the configuration of intrinsically chiral networks by enantioselective self-assembly.
Representative examples of these five approaches will be presented thereafter in order to underline the advantages and drawbacks of each strategy.
The second part of the text will deal with the specific physico-chemical properties that have been experienced or that can be forecast in ECM. In a first section, the influence of the enantiomeric purity on the magnetic properties of the materials will be reviewed. A crucial consequence of the enantiopurity of the described compounds is their crystallisation in one of the 65 chiral space groups (Sohncke groups). The loss of centrosymmetry is at the origin of the appearance of the well-known natural circular dichroism (NCD) but, depending on the space group of the material, it is also an open door for numerous interesting optical and electrical properties.27,28 In the former case, the main ones are the aforementioned NCD and second harmonic generation (SHG). In the latter case, the main ones are piezoelectricity, pyroelectricity and ferroelectricity (FE). Through the interaction with the magnetic properties (Fig. 1(c)), these properties can give birth to highly desirable original physical properties like magneto-chiral dichroism (MChD), magnetisation-induced SHG (MSHG) or multiferroicity (Fig. 4). Following these possibilities, the results obtained in the fields of MChD and MSHG will be presented. Finally, the ferroelectric materials obtained from resolved building blocks are described as a perspective towards molecular multiferroics.
## 2. Strategies towards enantiopure chiral magnets (ECM)
## 2.1 Spontaneous resolution process
This approach can hardly be considered as a rational strategy or, at least, as an a priori method. It indeed relies on a benchmark of the structures of existing systems in order to identify those exhibiting a spontaneous resolution during the crystallisation process. Nevertheless three families of compounds will be described in this section because they played an important role in the development of chiral magnets.
The first one is nickel(II) sulfate hexahydrate. This very simple compound, which is mere paramagnet down to $2\mathrm{~K~}$ , crystallises either in the $P4_{1}2_{1}2$ or $P4_{3}2_{1}2$ enantiomorphous space groups.29 It thus undergoes a spontaneous resolution with the formation of either right-handed or left-handed helices of hexaaquanickel(II) complexes hydrogen-bonded to sulfate ions. After selection of the handedness of the single crystals by NCD, the corresponding single crystals were used for the first demonstration of MCh dichroism (MChD) in the solid state.23 The importance of this result has motivated the chemists for developing a better control of the absolute configuration of the material. Moreover, they attempted to overcome the weakness of the magnetisation of this paramagnet by introducing LRMO.
A second compound that deserves to be described here is ${\mathrm{CsCuCl}}_{3}$ . The (re)determination of the crystal structure of this compound revealed that crystalises in the $P6_{1}22$ space group is composed of spiraling chains of face sharing distorted $\mathrm{CuCl}_{6}$ octahedra due to a helical atomic displacement of the copper(II) ions with respect to the $\delta_{1}$ $c$ -axis.30 It is thus structurally chiral. Interestingly, this compound appears to be relevant of $\mathbf{ABX}_{3}$ -type triangular lattice antiferromagnets with a N ´eel temperature of $10.7\mathrm{~K~}$ . The neutron diffraction study revealed that the spins of neighbouring copper(II) ions are rotated by $5.1^{\circ}$ along the chains while they are arranged in a $120^{\circ}$ structure in the planes perpendicular to the chains.11 The former phenomenon is related to the coexistence of ferromagnetic and antisymmetric Dzyaloshinsky–Moriya (DM) interaction along the chains (Fig. 2(d)). The structure in the $c$ -plane is a way to accommodate the frustration in the triangular antiferromagnetically (AF) coupled spin lattice(Fig. 2(c)). Such an organisation of the spins can thus be defined as magnetically chiral in both senses of the term. The occurrence of magnetic chirality is interesting because (i) it could be related to original critical behaviours at the transition temperature31,32 and (ii) it raises a possible link between the structural and magnetic chiralities (cf. Section 3.1).
A third series of compounds is particularly interesting. $\mathrm{[M(hfac)_{2}N I T P h O M e]}$ $(\mathbf{M}\ =\ \mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ , $\mathrm{{Co};\ h f a c\ =\ (1,1,1}$ ,5,5,5)- hexafluoroacetylacetonate; $\mathrm{\DeltaNITPhOMe}=2\cdot$ -(4-methoxyphenyl)- 4,4,5,5-tetramethylimidazoline-1-oxyl-3-oxide) have been obtained by reacting the nitronyl-nitroxide radical NITPhOMe with bis(hexafluoroacetylacetonate)metal(II), $[\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{hfac})_{2}]$ .33,34 The manganese(II) and cobalt(II) derivatives are isostructural. They crystallise in the enantiomorphous pair of space groups ${P3}_{1}$ or $P3_{2}$ according to the helicity of the compound. The structures are composed of one-dimensional (1D) helices where the cisbis(hexafluoroacetylacetonate)metal(II) moieties are connected by the nitronyl-nitroxide radicals (Fig. 5(a)). Compound with an absolute configuration $(A)$ (respectively (D)) of the metal centre crystallises in the ${P3}_{1}$ (respectively $P3_{2}$ ) space group. Consequently, the handedness of the metal-radical helices is $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ (respectively M).12,35 The exchange interaction between the different spins along the chain is strongly AF, leading to ferrimagnetic chains. In the case of cobalt(II), the single-ion anisotropy of the metal ion combined with strong exchange intrachain exchange interaction leads to the observation of a single-chain magnet (SCM) behaviour below $17\mathrm{~K~}$ .34 On the contrary, for the manganese(II) derivatives, the higher value of the overall spin radical together with the isotropic behaviour of the metal ion favours the appearance of a three-dimensional (3D) LRMO below $4.8~\mathrm{~K~}$ related to magnetic dipolar interaction. Interestingly, exploiting the noncentrosymmetry of the materials, the second harmonic generation (SHG) properties of the manganese(II) derivatives have been recorded leading to the observation of a sizeable SHG activity at $1064~\mathrm{{nm}}$ .33
## 2.2 Chiral bridging ligands
2.2.1 Chiral radicals associated with the metal-radical approach. The last result described in the previous paragraph underlines the interest to use enantiopure radical to build chiral molecule-based magnets through the metal-radical approach.38–42 By modifying the radical, it is indeed possible to insure that a chiral structure will be obtained rather than rely on a hypothetical spontaneous resolution of the materials.
In a first attempt to explore this possibility, the NITPhOMe radical has been replaced by a resolved bisaminoxyl diradical.36 In the compound obtained by reacting this radical with $[\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{hfac})_{2}]$ , the chiral diradical binds two trans-bis(hexafluoroacetylacetonate)manganese(II) moieties leading to a material crystalising in the $P1$ space group composed once again of 1D helices (Fig. 5(b)). Below $5.4\mathrm{K}$ , the compound presents an AF LRMO due to interactions between neighbouring chains. Furthermore, it behaves as a metamagnet, that is as an antiferromagnet that switches to a ferromagnet under a spinflip magnetic field of $50~\mathrm{mT}$ at $1.8\mathrm{~K~}$ .
This strategy has undoubtedly several advantages:
Since the radicals are organic molecules, this approach relies on the well documented synthesis of enantiopure molecules either through enantioselective organic synthesis or separation of the enantiomers of a racemic mixture, by chiral chromatography for example.
The intrachain exchange interaction between the radical and the metal ions is rather strong. It can indeed reach several hundreds of wavenumbers.
The main drawback of this approach is that, up to date, it has essentially led to 1D crystallographic structures. The LRMO is then governed by uncontrolled interchain exchange interactions and unavoidable dipolar interactions, much weaker in intensity than the aforementioned metal-radical exchange interactions. As a consequence, the ordering temperatures remain low and can hardly be rationalised since they are highly dependent on the crystal packing of complex species. Finally, it should be noted that, up to date, in all the published cases, the reactions have been carried out with only one enantiomer of the radicals.
2.2.2 Bridging ligands from the chiral pool. For both an easier synthetic access to the chiral ligand and a tentative increase of the dimensionality, the chiral pool, e.g. the stock of readily available enantiopure molecules, is a possibility that definitely deserved to be explored.
A first attempt was made starting from malate using hydrothermal conditions to build up extended networks.44 Homometallic and heterobimetallic $\left[\mathbf{M}(\mathrm{(L)}\mathrm{-mal})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})\right]$ $\mathbf{M}=$ Mn, Fe, Co, $Z\mathrm{n}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}_{0.63}\mathbf{C}\mathrm{o}_{0.37}$ , $\mathbf{Mn}_{0.79}\mathbf{Co}_{0.21}$ ; $\mathrm{(L)\mathrm{-mal}\ =\ \mathrm{(L)}}.$ - $\mathrm{HOOC-CH_{2}\mathrm{-}C H(O H)\mathrm{-}C O O H)}$ compounds are isostructural and crystallise in the $P2_{1}2_{1}2_{1}$ space group. In the structure, the metal ions are surrounded by six oxygen atoms, five of them being provided by the malate ion carboxylate moieties acting either as monodentate or bidentate ligands, the sixth one coming from a water molecule (Fig. 6(b–c)). The malate ligand thus bridges four metal ions together leading to the formation of a 3D coordination network. The exchange interaction between the metal ions is AF. The Mn derivatives is an antiferromagnet with a Ne´ el temperature of $3.5\mathrm{~K~}$ while $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}_{0.63}\mathbf{C}0_{0.37}$ and $\mathrm{Mn}_{0.79}\mathrm{Co}_{0.21}$ derivatives behave as weak ferromagnets with critical temperatures of 1.8 and $2.31\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively.
The same methodology has been applied with the (L)-tartrate anion. Hydrothermal treatment of solutions containing the metal ion (Mn, Fe, Co, Ni) associated with various anions and the chiral ligand has been performed.45 Single crystals were obtained for the cobalt(II) derivatives when starting from cobalt(II) oxalate, $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}){\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The $1:1$ compound then crystallises in the $I222$ space group. The repeating unit is a $(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{(L)}\mathrm{-tart})_{2})$ dimer (Fig. 6(d)). The $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ ions are in a six-fold oxygenated environment: two tartrate ligands are chelating the cobalt(II) ions with two oxygen atoms coming from one of the carboxylate group and from the neighbouring alcohol function while the two other oxygen atoms come from the second oxygen atom of the carboxylate group of different tartrate ions (Fig. 6(d)). The tartrate ions are thus acting as bridges both through the carboxylate groups and through the C–C bonds between the two equivalent parts of this chiral molecular ion. All the cobalt(II) centres present a (D) absolute configuration indicating a complete chiral transfer from the ligand to the metal.46,47 Finally the tartrate bridges lead to the formation of a 3D network that can be seen as 2D layers built up by the carboxylate bridges held together in the third direction by the carbon backbone of the tartrate ligands. By using the other first-row transition metals ions, powder samples were obtained. It has been demonstrated by powder X-ray diffraction that the other compounds of the series were isostructural to the cobalt derivatives. The weak exchange interactions are mediated by the syn, anti carboxylate bridges leading to an AF (respectively ferromagnetic (F)) exchange interaction between the manganese(II), iron(II) and cobalt(II) (respectively nickel(II)) ions. Below $3.3~\mathrm{~K~}$ , the manganese(II) derivatives behave as a canted antiferromagnet. Below $6~\mathrm{K}$ , the nickel(II) derivatives behave as a metamagnet with a spin-flip field of $0.3\mathrm{~T~}$ at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ complicated by a spin canting phenomenon in the AF phase below $4.5\mathrm{~K~}$ . The magnetic phase diagram (Fig. 6(e)) sums up this complex magnetic behaviour.
This approach has two particularities: (i) the ease of access to the chiral ligand; (ii) the use of solvothermal conditions that favour the increase of dimensionality. The following limitations can be put forward: (i) the second enantiomer of the chiral ligand is not always available; in turn, the authors of the aforedescribed papers only used the (L) enantiomer of the ligand; (ii) the synthesis of homometallic networks is the most accessible, favouring AF exchange interaction between the spin carriers. Concerning this last point, it should be noted that, thanks to the non-centrosymmetry of the compounds brought by the enantiopure chiral ligand, antisymmetric DM interaction is often at work in these systems leading to canted antiferromagnets rather than pure antiferromagnets and, hence, to the existence of a non-zero magnetisation in the LRMO phase. Nevertheless, the overall magnetisation remains weak, a factor which is not favourable for the observation of strong MCh effects for example. The authors have proposed two means to bypass the limitation brought about by the AF nature of the exchange interaction: (i) a proper choice of the metal ions to favour F exchange interaction;45 this strategy partly failed because of the AF dipolar coupling of the F-coupled 2D layers; (ii) a statistical mixing of the metal ions towards ferrimagnets;44 this strategy is very classical in solid state chemistry and had been extended to molecule-based materials;48–52 its efficiency and limitations have been demonstrated.
## 2.3 Association of chiral coligands with achiral connectors
Another possibility to increase the dimensionality of chiral magnetic networks and preserve their magnetic properties is to use bridging ligands that are known to favour the formation of 2D or 3D networks and transmit the exchange interaction. Nevertheless, none of the usual ligands are chiral. In order to introduce controlled chirality in the networks, it is necessary to use a chiral coligand in an enantiopure form. In the most favourable cases, such a ligand is able to transfer its chirality to the metal centre.46,47
Another popular ligand towards high dimensionality molecule-based magnets is the azide ion.58–61 Apart from its coordination versatility, this ligand is often used in the presence of a coligand. By making this coligand chiral,62,63 it is possible to synthesise ECM. For example by using $(R)$ - or (S)-1-phenylethylamine, a copper(II)-based chiral ferromagnet with a Curie temperature of $5.5\mathrm{~K~}$ was obtained.63 In this compound, the copper(II) ions are bridged by end-on azide ligands. One of the metal ions is further coordinated by two 1-phenylethylamine ligands. Neutral 2D brick-wall coordination layers are formed and held together by $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions and H-bonds.
Amino acids such as (D)- and (L)-aminoalanine $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -alaH) were also used with $\mathrm{[Cr^{III}(C N)_{6}]^{3-}}$ and manganese(II) ions in alkaline conditions to afford the crystallisation of $[(\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{CN})_{6})$ - $(\mathrm{Mn\mathrm{-}(D)\mathrm{-}N H_{2}\mathrm{-}a l a)_{3}]\cdot3H_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{\small{[(Cr^{III}(C N)_{6})(M n-(L)-N H_{2}-a l a)_{3}]}}$ $3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the $P6_{3}$ space group.55 The crystal structure is rather complex. The manganese(II) ions are bridged together by amino acid derivatives acting as bis(bidente) bridging ligands leading to the formation of triple helical homometallic strands along the $c$ -axis. These strands generate a chiral channel occupied by disordered water molecules. Moreover they are bound together by $\mathrm{[Cr^{III}(C N)_{6}]^{3-}}$ complexes acting as ligands leading to the formation of a 3D network (Fig. 7(c)). The compound orders as a ferrimagnet below $35\mathrm{~K~}$ .
Coupling an achiral bridge with a chiral coligand has many advantages. It relies on using short bridges $(\mathrm{CN}^{-},~\mathrm{N_{3}}^{-})$ which are efficient to transmit a controlled exchange interaction and chiral coligand that can be varied at will. The main drawbacks are related to the introduction of the chiral coligand. This partner deeply modifies the structure of the obtained materials compared to the parent achiral ones. It is thus difficult to predict the formation and the topology of the coordination network and/or to anticipate the ease of crystallisation of the compound. Moreover, since the chiral coligand occupies the coordination position of the bridging ligand, it reduces the connectivity of the magnetic network and, hence, the temperatures where LRMO occurs. Finally, surprisingly, there is not a systematic chiral transfer from the ligand to the paramagnetic coordinated metal centres and this can be a limitation for the observation of strong MCh effects.
## 2.4 Enantioselective self-assembly
The oxalate ligand is another unavoidable ligand in molecular magnetism. Its interest to obtain chiral magnets is that two (in a cis-manner) or three oxalate ions can bind a metal ion inducing a metal-based helical chirality (Fig. 2(b)). In the following, the latter case, e.g. chiral magnets made solely from tris(oxalato)metalate moities, will be detailed.
In contrast with the cyanide ligand, oxalate ions have initially been used to obtain discrete entities.64–67 These multimetallic complexes were studied in order to understand and quantify the influence of the interactions between the magnetic orbitals on the exchange interaction. In the case of tris(oxalato)metalate(III), it was only in 1992 that a series of ferromagnets of general formula $\mathrm{NBu}_{4}[\mathrm{M}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]$ ( $\mathrm{\DeltaBu~}=$ $n{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{9}$ , $\mathbf{M}\ =\ \mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ , Fe, Ni, Cu) was published.68 Their Curie temperatures were ranging between $6{\:}\mathrm{K}$ $\mathbf{\mathbf{M}}=\mathbf{\mathbf{M}}\mathbf{\mathbf{n}})$ and $14\mathrm{K}$ $(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Ni})$ ). This family was rapidly extended to other couples of metal ions leading to para-, antiferro-, ferri- or ferromagnetic compounds with critical temperatures that can reach $45\mathrm{~K~}$ for $\mathrm{Fe(II){\mathrm{-}}F e(\mathrm{III)}}$ derivatives.49,69–72
The crystallographic structures, confirmed by a topological analysis, revealed that $\mathrm{C}[\mathbf{M}_{1}\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]$ compounds where the two metal ions are in a tris(bidente) environment can build either 3D (10,3) or 2D (6,3) coordination networks (Fig. 9). The formation of such networks have been reviewed,73–75 so that only the main features of these family of materials will be recalled together with recently appeared results. The first feature of $\mathrm{C}[\mathbf{M}_{1}\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]$ compound is that, following the connectivity of the tris(oxalato)metalate(III) moiety, the connectivity of the network is three, independently of its dimensionality. The second feature is that there is a one-to-one correspondence between the relative configuration of adjacent coordinated metal ions and the dimensionality of the coordination network (Fig. 9): if the relative configurations are opposite, e.g. (L, D) or $(A,A)$ , the coordination network will be 2D; if they are identical, e.g. $(A,4)$ or $(A,A)$ , the network will be 3D. The third feature is that the counter-ion associated with the formation of 3D (10,3) coordination networks are essentially tris(dimine)metal complexes. This is due to the occurrence of strong $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions between the coordination anionic network and the counter-ion.76 This interaction can be exploited to build a 3D network starting from ‘‘naked’’ metal ions and free oxalate ions77 even when the metal ion embedded in the coordination network generally favours other geometries over the six-fold environment imposed by the three oxalate ligands.52,78
As far as ECM are concerned, it rapidly appeared that the formation of the 3D (10,3) compounds is based on a spontaneous resolution process leading to the formation of single crystals where the metal ions of the coordination network as well as the tris(diimine) metal ion have the same absolute configuration (Fig. 9). Nevertheless, as underlined in Section 2.1, this process cannot be considered as a rational strategy towards ECM since one cannot control the absolute configuration of the coordinated metal ions a priori. A possible way to obtain enantiopure oxalate-based magnets could have been to use chiral coligand to impose the absolute configuration of the coordinated metal ions. Nevertheless the decrease of connectivity would have led to an undesirable decrease of the LRMO temperatures.
Unfortunately, it is not possible to obtain enantiopure magnets as single crystals starting from resolved tris(oxalato)- metalate(III) complexes because the racemisation process is much faster than the crystal growth. Another strategy, based on a careful analysis of the structure, was thus proposed to obtain enantiopure 3D oxalate-based networks. It exploits the homochirality of the metal centres and the tight contacts between the cations and the anionic coordination network. Starting from resolved tris(diimine)ruthenium(II) cations, which are configurationally stable in solution, it is indeed possible to grow enantiopure single crystals of the 3D LRMO compounds by enantioselective self-assembly of the coordination network.35,79 As far as MCh effects are concerned, this strategy has a severe limitation: the tris(diimine)ruthenium(II) cations exhibit very intense MLCT transitions in the visible range. Though MCD was measured in $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{2}(\mathrm{ppy)}][\mathrm{MnCr(ox)}_{3}]$ $(\mathrm{bpy}\ =\ 2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine; ppyH $=\ 2$ -phenylpyridine),35 this contribution strongly reduces the relative impact of the MCh effects on the overall absorption of the material.
An elegant way to overcome this problem is to perform enantioselective self-assembly of 2D oxalate-based compounds. The synthetic challenge is greatly enhanced by the heterochiral arrangement of the metal ion of the 2D (6,3) coordination network. As for the chiral radical described in Section 2.2, one can rely on (i) the richness of enantioselective organic chemistry and (ii) the configurational stability of organic cation to synthesise ECM. In this line, three families of ammonium cations were explored:
chiral tetraalkyl ammonium with four different alkyl chains, e.g. with nitrogen-centred chirality;80,81 chiral ferrocenic ammonium cations exhibiting planar chirality (Fig. 9);82  tetraalkyl ammonium with one chiral alkyl chain, e.g. with carbon-centred chirality (Fig. 2(a) and 9).83,84
For the first family, it was not possible to separate the two enantiomers of the chiral cations and, hence, enantioselective self-assembly from resolved template cations could not be tested.81 The chiral ferrocenic ammonium cations were obtained in their enantiopure form by asymmetric cyclopalladation. The enantioselective self-assembly led to excellent results for powdered samples.82 Nevertheless the hydrolysis of the cation during the crystallisation process prevented the enantioselective self-assembly of single crystals.85 Finally, the tetraalkyl ammonium with one chiral alkyl chain, obtained by quaternisation of the resolved 2-aminobutane,86 appeared as a good chiral templating agent towards the formation of enantiopure 2D (6,3) oxalate-based materials $\begin{array}{r}{[\mathbf{N}((S)–i–\mathbf{B}\mathrm{u})\mathbf{MePr}_{2}][(A)\mathbf{Mn}-}\end{array}$ (D)- $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]$ (S)-5 $\mathbf{\langle}i\mathbf{-Bu}=\mathrm{CH}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{5}$ ; $\mathbf{Me}=\mathbf{CH}_{3}$ ; $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{Pr}=\mathbf{C}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{7},}\end{array}$ ) and $[\mathrm{N}((R)\ –i\mathrm{-Bu})\mathrm{MePr}_{2}][(\varDelta)\ –\mathrm{Mn}(\varLambda)\ –\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]$ $(R){-}5$ (Fig. 9) which crystallise in the $P6_{3}$ space group.83 The analysis of the crystal structure of the enantiopure single crystals reveals that the enantioselective self-assembly process is tentatively governed by the Van der Waals interaction between the chiral alkyl chain and one of the oxalate paddles of the tris(oxalato)- chromate(III) moiety leading to a discrimination between the two enantiomers of the coordination building blocks present in solution. In the case of the use of $\left[\mathrm{N}((S){\cdot}\mathrm{CH}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})\mathrm{Ph})\mathrm{Me}_{3}\right]^{+}$ ,84 the situation is not as clear because (i) the self-assembly has been performed with only one enantiomer of the template cation, (ii) no NCD data are available, (iii) the detailed examination of the crystal structure indicates that the chiral centre is, within the experimental errors, at the same distance from the two metal ions discarding preferential intermolecular interactions with one of the two members of the heterochiral pair. Finally, both enantiomers of 5 behave as ferromagnets with a Curie temperature of $7\mathrm{~K~}$ . Since the ammonium cation does not absorb in the visible region, the single crystals of (S)-5 and (R)-5 fulfil all the requirements for the observation of MCh effects (cf. Section 3.2).
This elegant strategy takes advantage of the native coexistence of chirality and magnetism in the parent compounds. Moreover, since the enantioselectivity of the reaction is insured through intermolecular interactions, the topology and the connectivity of the coordination network and, hence, its magnetic properties, are preserved. The first drawback is related to the weakness of the enantioselective intermolecular interactions. It might thus be difficult to generalise the occurrence of the enantioselective self-assembly though it was successful for the 3D oxalate-based compounds and for the two resolved ammonium cations tested for the 2D oxalate-based materials. In the specific case of this latter family, the second limitation is the possibility to obtain single crystals. Throughout the literature on this wide family of compounds, it is indeed limited to the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mn-Co}$ and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}{-}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}$ derivatives which are, respectively, paramagnets and ferromagnets with the lowest $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ within the family.
## 3. Physico-chemical properties of enantiopure chiral magnets
## 3.1 Magnetic properties: the missing link
It is interesting to figure out whether the enantiopurity of a chiral magnet has any influence on its magnetic properties. The most frequently described influence is the appearance of canted AF LRMO instead of pure antiferromagnetism.44,45,72,78 It appears when antiparallel magnetic moments (AF coupling) are slightly tilted in the perpendicular direction by antisymmetric DM exchange interaction. The influence of DM interaction is indeed most evident when $J$ is negative because the appearance of a purely AF LRMO leads to a steep decrease of the magnetic susceptibility at the Ne´ el temperature $T_{\mathrm{N}}$ while it slightly increases when DM interaction is present.
For ferromagnets or ferrimagnets, the thermal variation of the magnetisation around $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ cannot be simply exploited to evidence the presence of DM interaction. Because of the magnetic moment borne by the neutron, neutron diffraction can probe directly the spin organisation of materials. Accurate determinations of the magnetic structures rely on using either big single crystals or deuterated powdered samples, hardly available for molecular magnets. Nevertheless some efforts have been made in this direction. The magnetic contribution to the diffracted signal in deuterated 3D oxalate-based magnets $[\mathrm{Ru(d_{8}{\cdot}b p y)_{2}(d_{8}{\cdot}p p y)}][\mathrm{MnCr(ox)_{3}}]$ was reproduced by a parallel arrangement of the magnetic moments without introducing any tilt angle between them.87 In the case of 2, the comparison between the experimental data and the calculations for different spin structures indicates that ‘‘the magnetic structure might be collinear with the magnetic moments along the $a$ -axis, or it might be noncollinear and conical with the magnetic moments nearly along the $a$ -axis and the apex of the cone being less than $20^{\circ}\ '$ .88 Muon spin relaxation studies have been performed to distinguish between the two possibilities.89 The most striking result of this latter study is that, because the measurements performed on the two enantiomers are identical, $i f$ the magnetic structure is conical, the two enantiomers have opposite absolute configuration on both crystallographic and magnetic points of view (Fig. 2). These studies underline how difficult it is to determine precisely the magnetic structures of enantiopure chiral magnets. Nevertheless, such studies must be pursued on the other synthesised compounds (cf. Section 2) in order to firmly established the link between magnetic and structural chiralities (Fig. 2) and identify the parameters that govern it.
Another specificity in the magnetic properties of ECM is the possible appearance at low temperature of a chiral collective spin excitation named chiral spin soliton.90,91 Once again, the helicity of the spin soliton appears because of the presence of DM interaction. Such solitons have been evidenced by Electron Spin Resonance (ESR) on 2. The influence of the enantiopurity of the material has been clearly evidenced since these chiral excitations are not observed in the corresponding racemic compounds but no effect of the absolute configuration on the orientation of the helices has been reported. Finally, a theoretical study forecasts that the presence of chiral soliton should influence the thermal variation of the magnetisation below $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ with the appearance of a characteristic cusp when the field is applied perpendicular to the chiral axis (Fig. 10(a)).92 The simulated curves clearly indicate that this cusp is related to the non-zero value of the DM interaction.
In all the previous results, it has been shown that the enantiopurity of chiral magnets influences the magnetically ordered state. The DM interaction can also influence the system when it is not magnetically ordered. In turn, a giant non-linearity of the ac magnetic susceptibility has been observed in 2 just above the Curie temperature.93 The third harmonic of the ac magnetic susceptibility $\chi_{3\omega}$ is indeed connected to the breaking of spatial symmetry. In the present case, its in-phase component $\chi_{3\omega}^{\quad\prime}$ reaches $24\%$ of the value of the fundamental term $\chi_{1\omega}^{\quad\prime}$ just above $T_{\mathrm{C}}=37~\mathrm{K}$ for the $(R)$ enantiomer while it is vanishingly small for the racemic compound (Fig. 10(b)). The authors did not explicitly state that $(R)$ and $(S)$ were checked to be identical.
These studies are important because the magnetic properties were believed to be independent of the enantiomeric purity of the compound. In turn, the breaking of the spatial symmetry reduces the symmetry of the exchange interaction. The antisymmetric term is then allowed and directly influences the magnetic properties of the materials. New phenomena appear in the enantiopure compounds compared to their racemic analogs. Nevertheless, despite the effort made by neutrons diffraction87,88 and muon spectroscopy,89 it has not been possible to definitely differentiate the magnetic structures of the two enantiomers.
## 3.2 Magnetochiral effects
The evocation of chiral objects is tightly related to their specific optical properties and, in particular, NCD which is related to natural optical activity by the Kramers–Koenig relations. Experimentally, NCD is characterised by a differential absorption of left (LCP) and right (RCP) circularly polarised light by an enantiomerically pure or, by at least, enriched, chiral medium (Fig. 11(a)). This effect is related to the breaking of the spatial symmetry of the medium.
An effect with strong phenomenological similarities, called magnetic circular dichroism (MCD), is present in magnetic medium: LCP and RCP are differentially absorbed by the medium. The effect is governed by the intensity and orientation of the magnetisation component parallel to the wave vector of the light k (Fig. 11(b)). It is related to the breaking of the timeinversion symmetry of the medium.
In the mid-1980s, a cross-effect has been forecast by developing the electric permittivity tensor with respect to $\mathbf{k}$ and B, the applied magnetic field,20,21 in much the same way to what can be done to explain non-linear optical properties by making such a development with the power of the electric field E of the incident light. As expected, a cross-term noted $\gamma(\omega)\mathbf{k}\mathbf{B}$ then appears. It is characterised by a third rank tensor $\gamma(\omega)$ that is non-zero only if the medium is optically active AND its magnetisation is non-zero, e.g. when there is a simultaneous breaking of space AND time-inversion symmetries in the medium. Following this double symmetry breaking, the so-called magneto-chiral dichroism (MChD) is characterised by differential absorption when an enantiopure magnetised sample is crossed by unpolarised light when its magnetisation is reversed (Fig. 11(c)). Conversely, the effect can also be reversed by using the other enantiomer of the chiral object. The experimental confirmation of the existence of this effect came at the end of the last century on diamagnetic or paramagnetic medium submitted to external magnetic fields.22–24 The MCh effects then represent a few thousandths of the total signal. This is related both to the second-order origin of this cross-effect and to the weakness of the magnetisation of the sample. It is thus possible to enhance the signal by using a medium with higher magnetisation. An elegant way to perform this increase is to use ferro- or ferri-magnets. Moreover, the possible bistability of the magnetically ordered phase is an invitation to use the MCh effect to read out the bit of information stored in the local orientation of the magnetisation of the medium as it has been done by MCD.94
As recalled in Introduction, the search for the increase of the MChD upon magnetic ordering has been a powerful motivation to accept the challenge of synthesising enantiopure chiral magnets. To figure out the possible candidates to observe this effect among those described in Section 2, it is interesting to list the requirements that the ideal candidate must fulfil:
Following the required conditions for observing NCD, the material must be not only chiral but the sample must be enantiomerically pure or, at least, enriched, in order to exhibit an optical activity on polarised light. Moreover, measuring the effect on the two enantiomers is an important feature to rule out any ambiguity on the origin of this small effect.
As already mentioned, a magnetisation as high as possible is desirable to enhance the effect.
 A third condition does not directly appears in the classical theory of MCh effects20,21,95 but can be considered as a reasonable conjecture: following the highly localised light–matter interaction, it can be conjectured that MCh effects will be greater if the chiral and magnetic centres are the same.
To avoid that the rather weak MCh effects to be blurred by the absorption of the sample, they should be observed in a spectral region where no diamagnetic and/or achiral centres interact with light.
As MChD is measured in the transmission mode,22 it is highly desirable to obtain the two enantiomers of the targeted chiral magnets as thin transparent single crystals.
Several compounds described in Section 2 fulfil these requirements. Nevertheless, up to date, only one has been used for MChD studies, that is (S)-5 and (R)-5.83 The spectral variations of the MChD have been measured for both enantiomers of this ferromagnet. As expected from the theoretical prediction, the two spectra are mirror images of one another with respect to the wavelength axis (Fig. 12(a)). As already observed in nickel(II) sulfate hexahydrate,22 its shape is reminiscent of the first derivatives of the NCD signal. The thermal variation of the MChD at the wavelength where it is maximum $(615\ \mathrm{nm})$ shows an abrupt increase of the signal when it crosses $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ towards lower temperatures (Fig. 12(b)). This variation closely follows the variation of the magnetisation confirming this parameter as a key-factor in the intensity of the effect for a given chirality.
The perspectives opened by these pioneering measurements are numerous. First of all, a better understanding of the effect would strongly benefit from the study of the MChD in the other enantiopure magnets described in Section 2. Varying systems would indeed allow figuring out what are the different factors governing the effect. This experimental approach could be nicely completed by a theoretical description going beyond the classical description already proposed.95 By using a material with higher coercive force, it should be demonstrated that MChD can be used for the read-out of the information encoded in the remnant orientation of the magnetisation of the material. One of the aims of molecular magnetism is to store and read the information stored on one single object as single-molecule magnets $\left(\mathrm{SMM}\right)^{96,97}$ or single-chain magnets (SCM). By using enantiopure $\mathrm{SMM^{98}}$ and SCM,99 MChD can appear as a probe for such systems.
## 3.3 Further cross-effects with enantiopure magnets
By exhibiting a cross-effect like MChD, enantiopure chiral magnets appear as a clear example of multifunctional magnetic molecule-based materials where the presence of two adequate properties creates a third one (Fig. 1(c)). The loss of centrosymmetry is at the origin of the appearance of NCD but it is also an open door for numerous interesting optical and electrical properties (Fig. 4).27,28 Though it is not the unique route, using resolved chiral precursors might be considered a straightforward and thus rational way to obtain non-centrosymmetric materials exhibiting SHG and/or ferroelectricity.
3.3.1 Magnetisation Induced Second Harmonic Generation (MSHG). There are several ways to induce SHG in a material. A versatile one developed by physicists is to prepare centrosymmetric compounds as thin layers or as multilayers. The symmetry breaking at the interface allows the appearance of SHG light which is influenced by the magnetisation of the material leading to MSHG. The initial work on metallic layers have been transposed to electrochemically grown trimetallic $(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Cr}_{1-x})[\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]{\cdot}7.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ PBA.100,101 Surprisingly since the powdered material is cubic, the thin layer exhibits the SHG signal from the bulk and not only from the interfaces. Moreover, it is enhanced when the material enters in its ferrimagnetic phase and its variations are correlated to those of the Field Cooled magnetisation. This is a clear evidence of the presence of MSHG. Nevertheless, as in the cases of spontaneous resolution described in Section 2.1, this strategy cannot be generalised easily and cannot be considered as a rational one. On the contrary, the space group observed in (S)-5 $(\mathrm{P}6_{3})$ , a compound obtained by enantioselective selfassembly, makes this material a potential candidate for the observation of SHG and, in its ferromagnetic state, of MSHG. This possibility has indeed been tested successfully.102 The SHG signal is present and increases upon temperature decreasing below the Curie temperature of the material. Moreover, upon magnetisation reversal at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ , the MSHG extrema are shifted by $13.1^{\circ}$ . This value must be compared to angular variation of the order of $1^{\circ}$ used for reading out the magnetic information by linear magneto-optics.94,103 This makes MSHG, together with MChD, an alternative way to probe the magnetisation state of enantiopure chiral magnets. Finally, the single crystalline state of the sample allows performing a complete symmetry-based analysis of the nonlinear susceptibility tensor in the paramagnetic and ferromagnetic phases of the material. It is thus possible to understand the variation of the MSHG with the orientation of the magnetic field and/or of the angle between the polarisation of the incident radiation and the outgoing SHG signal.102
3.3.3 Multiferroics. A new family of multifunctional materials, named multiferroics, currently creates a growing interest.104 Such compounds possess two or even three ferroic orders, that is ferroelectricity, ferroelasticity and ferromagnetism. All these properties result from cooperative phenomena leading to the long range ordering of electric dipoles, deformations vectors of the crystalline network and local magnetic moments, respectively. One of the challenges is to couple the electric and magnetic ordering of the material. It would then be possible to control the electric polarisation applying an external magnetic field or to control the orientation of the magnetisation applying an external electric field (Fig. 1(e)). This latter potentiality would allow to use multiferroics for data storage and spintronics applications.105 Multiferroics are interesting even if the ferroelectricity and the ferromagnetism merely coexist (Fig. 1(a)) since such materials could be used as four-levels memories.106,107
Multiferroics represent a challenging synthetic target for both solid state and molecular chemists. It is currently difficult to propose a rational approach towards such materials since the coupling mechanisms of ferroelectric and ferromagnetic orders are still unknown or controversial. The scarce examples of multiferroics are metal oxides derived from the peroskites $\mathbf{ABO}_{3}$ .108 Nevertheless, it appears that the presence of d and/or f unpaired electrons creating the magnetic properties hinders the charge separation giving birth to the ferroelectric distorsion.109 Moreover, the symmetry requirements to observe ferroelectric ordering are severe and difficult to forecast.
The versatility of organic and coordination chemistry represents a possible solution to overcome these difficulties. In hybrid molecular materials, the expected properties can be brought independently by a proper choice of the constituting building blocks while the crystalline symmetry and the coupling between the properties can be controlled through crystal engineering and molecular and supramolecular interactions. The origins of ferroelectricity in molecule-based materials can be found in charge displacement, dipole reorientation or H-bonds reorganisation. These three possibilities lead to an impressive choice of starting materials.110 It should be noted that, in contrast with NCD or MChD, ferroelectric properties require a non-centrosymmetric space group (Fig. 4) but do not depend on the absolute configuration of the compound. Therefore, compounds obtained by spontaneous resolution from achiral or racemic chiral precursors as those presented in Section 2.1 are indeed interesting candidates for molecular multiferroics. One of these deserves a special attention since it is a PBA that, by a symmetry lowering when the temperature is decreased, exhibits both ferroelectric and ferromagnetic properties.111 Nevertheless, the serendipitous nature of this synthetic pathway remains. Given the scope of this review, the strategy based on enantiopure building blocks will be described in more details. The results are rather scarce but follow the strategies described in Section 2 to obtain ECM.112
The central idea is indeed to use chiral ligands either alone or in association with a (bridging) coligand.
As a first example, by reacting rare earth chloride with a $\upbeta$ -diketonate and $(R,R)$ -1,2-diamino-1,2-diphenyl-ethane, neutral discrete complexes have been obtained.113 As expected from the use of an enantiopure chiral ligand, they crystallise in the chiral space group $P2_{1}$ . The dielectric measurements revealed that the relative dielectric permittivity varies from 40 along the $c$ -axis to 240 along the $a$ -axis. This anisotropic behaviour is correlated to the relative orientation of the two independent molecules present in the asymmetric unit with respect to the crystallographic directions. Finally, the polarisation $\mathbf{P}$ versus electric field $\mathbf{E}$ curves exhibit a hysteretic behaviour at room temperature with a saturation value of $0.39\upmu\mathrm{C}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ which is much smaller than the value of $5.0~\upmu\mathrm{C}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ reached in $\mathrm{KH_{2}P O_{4}}$ (KDP). Using a more sophisticated enantiopure chiral ligand, namely $(S,S,S)\ –1,1^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime}\ –2,4$ ,6-trimethylbenzene-1,3,5-triyltris(methylene)-tris-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid 6, a homometallic $\left[\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\upmu_{3}\mathrm{-O})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}(\mathbf{6})_{3}\right]^{4-}$ complex where the nickel(II) ions are binded by a oxo ligand and by the carboxylate group of the ligand has been obtained.114 The absolute values and the anisotropy of the dielectric permittivity are most comparable to those reported for the previous compound while the electrical polarisation is much smaller. The polarisation versus electric field behaviour is much improved in the tetracoordinated trichloro( $(R)$ -2-methylpiperazinium)cobalt(II) complex: the saturation is reached and the saturation value is higher than those of KDP.115
Using ligands from the chiral pool (cf. Section 2.2.2) has been explored by reacting europium $\left(\mathrm{III}\right)^{117}$ and terbium $\left(\mathrm{III}\right)^{116}$ perchlorate with (L)-ethyllactate ((L)-EtLact) under hydrothermal conditions. The resulting materials are 2D compounds of formula $\mathrm{\small{[Ln((L)-EtLact)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}][C l O_{4}]}}$ $\begin{array}{r l r}{({\bf L}{\bf n}}&{{}=}&{{\bf E u}(\mathrm{III}).}\end{array}$ ,117 $\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{III}))^{116}$ where the metal ions are coordinated by six oxygen atoms from four different ethyllactate ligands, the coordination sphere being completed by two water molecules (Fig. 13(a)). For the europium(III) derivatives, the relative dielectric permittivity rises from 14 along the $a$ -axis to 2100 along the $b$ -axis exhibiting a huge anisotropy (Fig. 13(b)).117 At room temperature, the electrical polarisation reaches 0.1 mC cm2 for the europium(III) derivatives and $1.0~\upmu\mathrm{C}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for the terbium(III) analog (Fig. 13(c)).116 Though a fully ferromagnetic exchange interaction between the terbium(III) ions was reported, no LRMO occurs in this compound.
The association of an achiral bridging ligand with a chiral coligand has also been explored. By the association of (methyltris(pyrazolyl)borate)tri(cyanido)ferrate(III) with the nickel(II) ions and either enantiomers of 3 (cf. Section 2), bimetallic trinuclear complexes were obtained where the environment of the nickel(II) ion is most comparable to the situation encountered in 4 (cf. Section 2.3).118 There is a noticeable ferromagnetic interaction between the spin bearers of the complex. Moreover, the compound crystallises in the chiral space group $P2_{1}$ and it exhibits a hysteretic behaviour of the electrical polarisation when submitted to an external electric field. Finally, by reacting copper(II) acetate with dicyanamide ions and $(R)$ - or (S)-1-pyridyl-ethan-1-ol, it has been possible to obtain a 2D compound crystallising in the $P2_{1}$ space group.119 In the layers, 1-pyridyl-ethan-1-olate behaves as a bidentate ligand and, in addition, the alkoxo group bridges two copper atoms leading to the formation of a $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ cores. These cores are bridged by two dicyanimide bridges. The exchange interaction between the copper atoms is strongly AF while it is negligible through the dicyanimide bridges. Thus, despite the 2D coordination network, no LRMO is observed. On the contrary, an electric polarisation signal is observed under electric field but the saturation value of $0.002~\upmu\mathrm{C}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ is much weaker than those previously described.
## 4. Conclusions
The measurement of MCh effects in diamagnetic and paramagnetic chiral compounds has launched an intense activity towards the challenging synthesis of enantiopure chiral magnets. To reach this goal, chemists have used all the tools offered by the versatility of molecular chemistry, relying on chiral radicals, chiral coligands associated with achiral connectors or enantioselective self-assembly.
The well-characterised compounds have been used to study the influence of enantiomeric purity on the magnetic properties. The enhancement of the MChD upon entering in a ferromagnetically ordered phase has been evidenced. Finally, it appears that the non-centrosymmetry brought into the materials by using enantiopure chiral building blocks can be used towards a rational synthesis of materials exhibiting SHG and/or ferroelectric properties. It has been demonstrated that the association in such a material of SHG and LRMO leads to strong MSHG. A lot remains to be done to fully understand and exploit the original physico-chemical properties of ECM. Moreover, this demanding synthetic route must be further explored towards molecular multiferroics.
